white is accepted. Office walls with posters or curtains are rejected.\n• Group / cropped face — face must occupy 70–80% of the frame, both ears visible, neutral expression, no smile that bares teeth.\n• Signature in pencil or blue gel pen on lined paper — use a black ballpoint on plain white paper, then scan or photograph.\n• Signature must be inside the box; do NOT sign across the photo on physical forms.\n\nSpecification table (quick reference):\n\nField         | Requirement\n------------- | --------------------------------\nPhoto size    | 213 × 213 pixels (3.5 × 2.5 cm)\nPhoto weight  | 20 KB to 50 KB\nPhoto format  | JPEG\nBackground    | white\nSignature size| signature on white paper, 2 × 4.5 cm — 300 DPI\nSignature wt  | 10 KB to 20 KB\nSignature fmt | JPEG\nIssuing body  | Income Tax Department / NSDL / UTIITSL\nPortal        | onlineservices.nsdl.com / pan.utiitsl.com"}, {"heading": "Privacy, security and why VexaTool is safe for government forms", "body_md": "Your PAN Card (Form 49A / 49AA) photograph is sensitive personal data. VexaTool processes the image entirely in your browser using the HTML5 Canvas API — the file never leaves your device, is never uploaded to any server, and is not stored, logged or shared. Closing the tab erases everything. There are no accounts, no email capture, and no watermarks on the output. This makes the tool safe to use on shared computers in a cyber cafe and compliant with the Income Tax Department / NSDL / UTIITSL''s expectation that the candidate himself or herself prepares the file."}, {"heading": "Acceptable formats, EXIF and colour profiles", "body_md": "Most Indian government portals accept JPEG only. PNG is rejected at IBPS, SBI, SSC and UPSC, even though the file is smaller, because their image validator expects baseline JPEG markers. VexaTool always writes a baseline JPEG with the sRGB colour profile and no EXIF metadata — this is the safest combination across every portal we have tested.\n\nIf your source is HEIC (iPhone) or AVIF (modern Android), the tool decodes it transparently. If your source is a PDF page, use the PDF-to-JPG converter first, then bring the JPG here. If your source is a screenshot, retake the photo — screenshots include device UI chrome and are almost always rejected by face-detection validators.\n\nFinally, a note on Adobe RGB and CMYK: studio cameras sometimes embed these wider colour profiles. They look identical on screen but trigger rejections on older portals. VexaTool strips them on export, so the output is universally accepted."}]'::jsonb, faqs = '[{"q": "Will the resized photo be accepted by the PAN Card (Form 49A / 49AA) portal?", "a": "Yes — provided you keep the dimension at 213 × 213 pixels (3.5 × 2.5 cm), the format as JPEG, the file size inside 20 KB to 50 KB, and a white background. The portal validates these four properties; nothing else is checked at upload."}, {"q": "Why does my PAN Card (Form 49A / 49AA) application say ''invalid photograph'' even after resizing?", "a": "The most common cause is an extra colour-profile (CMYK / sRGB tag) added by mobile camera apps. VexaTool strips this on export. If the error persists, re-take the photo against a white wall — the Income Tax Department / NSDL / UTIITSL face-detection rejects busy backgrounds even when the file size is correct."}, {"q": "Is there a separate tool for the signature?", "a": "You can use the same resizer with the signature target preset: signature on white paper, 2 × 4.5 cm — 300 DPI, 10 KB to 20 KB, JPEG. Sign in black ink on plain white paper, photograph in good light, then crop tight around the signature before resizing."}, {"q": "How recent should the photograph be?", "a": "The Income Tax Department / NSDL / UTIITSL requires a photograph taken within the last three months. Signature must be inside the box; do NOT sign across the photo on physical forms."}, {"q": "Is the tool free and does it require sign-up?", "a": "VexaTool is completely free, has no account require